-- title:
--   Corollary 1.8.4 (Chow): the centre of $B_n$ is generated by $(\sigma_1\cdots\sigma_{n-1})^n$
-- statement:
--   **Corollary 1.8.4 (Chow).** For $n \ge 3$ the centre of the braid group $B_n$ is the cyclic subgroup generated by the full twist
--
--   $$\Delta^2 \;=\; (\sigma_1\sigma_2\cdots\sigma_{n-1})^n .$$
--
--   That is, an element of $B_n$ commutes with every braid if and only if it is an integral power of $(\sigma_1\cdots\sigma_{n-1})^n$. Geometrically the generator is the braid in which the whole bundle of $n$ strands is given one full rotation.
--
--   This is the equality-of-subgroups half of Corollary 1.8.4; the complementary assertion, that the generator has infinite order, so that the centre is infinite cyclic, follows from the exponent-sum homomorphism.
--
--   The result is the reason the centre plays no role in the conjugacy and word problems for $B_n$: quotienting by it loses only the number of full twists. The hypothesis $n \ge 3$ is necessary, since $B_2$ is infinite cyclic and equal to its own centre, whereas $(\sigma_1)^2$ generates a proper subgroup of it.
--
--   **Formalization Note** In the formal statement the generator is written as the $n$-th power of the ordered product of all the Artin generators, and the subgroup it generates is the set of its integer powers.
-- source:
--   Joan S. Birman, *Braids, Links, and Mapping Class Groups*, Annals of Mathematics Studies 82, Princeton University Press, 1974, Chapter 1, Corollary 1.8.4, p. 28 (due to W.-L. Chow, 1948)

import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ArtinBraidGroup
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ArtinEndo

namespace BraidsLinksMCG

theorem cor_1_8_4_center_eq (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) :
    Subgroup.center (ArtinBraidGroup n) = Subgroup.zpowers (sigmaProd n ^ n) := by sorry

end BraidsLinksMCG
